  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Add a Supplementary Shift to Distribute Load: Introduce a second partial shift (4–6 hours) staffed by cross-trained or on-call workers to absorb the incremental 20% demand. By distributing production hours across a larger workforce, individual fatigue accumulation is reduced and machine duty cycles (primary noise source) are spread across longer calendar time at lower intensity. No constraint limits need to be changed. Requires HR coordination and may incur overtime premium costs. Estimated lead time to implement: 24–48 hours. FatigueIndex is projected to fall to approximately 0.35 and Noise to approximately 78 dB under this configuration.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate): Engineering Controls + Job-Rotation Schedule: Deploy acoustic dampening panels or enclosures on the highest-noise workstations (targeting the 4.9 dB overshoot) and implement a mandatory job-rotation protocol every 90 minutes to cap individual FatigueIndex accumulation. These engineering and administrative controls can be enacted without relaxing constraint limits. Full demand can be attempted within the existing single shift, but scheduling complexity increases and there is a moderate risk of a residual deadline gap if rotation logistics slow throughput. Requires capital expenditure for noise controls (estimated 1–3 week procurement) and supervisor training for rotation enforcement.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 (Last Resort): Supervised Temporary Constraint Relaxation with Mandatory Mitigations: With explicit production manager sign-off and documented risk acceptance, temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.52 and the Noise ceiling to 83.0 dB for a single production run not to exceed 72 hours. Mandatory mitigations must accompany this authorization: (a) mandatory 30-minute rest breaks every 2 hours for all operators, (b) provision and enforced use of ANSI-rated hearing protection (NRR ≥ 25 dB) for all personnel on the floor, and (c) real-time monitoring with automatic halt if either parameter exceeds the temporarily relaxed limit. This option meets the full demand spike with no deadline gap but formally suspends human-centric constraint guarantees for the duration. An incident report and post-run ergonomic audit are required before returning to normal constraint levels.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
