    3. context.horizon_unit (shift duration unspecified)
       Q: What is the shift length in hours (e.g., 8 h, 10 h, 12 h)? A fatigue ceiling of 0.4 on a 12-hour shift implies a far tighter per-hour accumulation budget than on an 8-hour shift, and the feasibility of the whole contract changes accordingly.
       A: Assume an 8-hour shift with linear fatigue accumulation; maximum permissible fatigue rate is therefore 0.4 / 8 = 0.05 per hour. If actual shift length differs, the contract must be re-validated before the optimiser runs.
    4. resource_efficiency denominator — aggregation weights for energy, materials, labor-hours
       Q: How should energy consumption, raw-material consumption, and labor-hours be combined into a single resource-unit denominator — equal weights, cost-based weights, or CO₂-equivalent weights? Without this, the optimiser can game the metric by, e.g., compressing labor-hours while pushing pace (raising fatigue risk) if labor weighs heavily.
       A: Weight resources by their average direct cost share from the last fiscal quarter (energy : materials : labor in proportion to their cost fractions); if cost data are unavailable, use equal weights (1 : 1 : 1) normalized to the respective shift-average baseline.
    5. resource_efficiency numerator — minimum output floor (no lower bound on throughput)
       Q: Is there a minimum number of conforming units (or minimum total output value) that must be delivered this shift regardless of efficiency ratio? Without a floor, the optimiser can achieve a high value-per-resource ratio by running at very low throughput if marginal units are costly to produce.
       A: Enforce a minimum throughput equal to 80 % of the rolling 4-week shift average for the same product mix; if no historical baseline exists, set minimum output value at 75 % of the shift's planned production order value.

