    2. resource_scope / labor-hours in denominator of resource_efficiency
       Q: Since labor-hours appears in the resource denominator, the optimizer can improve resource_efficiency by reducing headcount for this shift — but fewer workers means the remaining operators carry more load, increasing per-person fatigue risk. Should labor-hours be excluded from the optimisable resource denominator and treated as a fixed input for this shift, or is staffing level a valid lever?
       A: Treat labor-hours as a fixed input (headcount is not a scheduling lever within a single shift); only energy and raw-material consumption are optimized in the denominator.
    3. operator_fatigue / constraint measurement timing
       Q: Does the fatigue ≤ 0.4 hard constraint apply continuously at every time-step throughout the shift (e.g., checked every 30 minutes), or only as a cumulative end-of-shift value? A burst-then-coast schedule could satisfy an end-of-shift reading while exposing operators to index values above 0.4 for hours mid-shift.
       A: Enforce the fatigue ≤ 0.4 constraint as a rolling ceiling checked at every scheduling interval (default: every 30 minutes), not only at shift end, to protect intra-shift wellbeing.
    4. priority_order vs. _raw_priority_order conflict
       Q: The contract's priority_order lists only 'HumanWellbeing', but the raw intent ranking places resource_efficiency first. When the optimizer can improve resource_efficiency by pushing fatigue from 0.35 to exactly 0.40 (still within the hard constraint), should it do so — or should it leave a safety buffer below 0.4 to prioritize human wellbeing over marginal efficiency gains?
       A: Apply a 10% safety buffer on the fatigue constraint (effective ceiling 0.36 rather than 0.40) when resource_efficiency improvements would otherwise consume the full headroom, consistent with the 'HumanWellbeing' priority declaration.
    5. delivered_value_metric / quality-adjustment weighting function
       Q: The delivered-value numerator is 'throughput quality-adjusted output units', but the quality weighting function is unspecified. If producing 100 high-quality units counts the same as 120 standard units, slowing cycle rate by 15% to hit quality targets may or may not improve the objective — can you provide the quality multiplier (e.g., 1 reject-free unit = X standard units) so the optimizer can evaluate that trade-off correctly?
