    1. priority_order vs _raw_priority_order
       Q: When maximising efficiency conflicts with operator fatigue (e.g., a high-value task pushes fatigue to 0.38–0.40), should the scheduler always sacrifice throughput to protect wellbeing, or is the efficiency ratio the primary objective as long as fatigue stays under 0.4?
       A: HumanWellbeing is treated as the lexicographic top priority: any schedule that reduces fatigue below the 0.4 hard limit is preferred over a higher-efficiency schedule that reaches it, even by 0.01.
    2. resource_types (aggregation weights for denominator)
       Q: How should labor-hours, kWh of energy, kg of raw material, and machine-hours be combined into a single 'resource_units' denominator — equal weight in normalised units, a cost-based currency weighting, or a CO₂-equivalent weighting?
       A: Each resource type is converted to a monetary cost at standard rates and summed; the denominator is total direct variable cost in the shift's base currency.
    3. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed (no minimum throughput floor)
       Q: If the optimiser can achieve a very high value-per-resource ratio by running only one premium, low-resource job and idling for the rest of the shift, is that acceptable, or is there a minimum production volume or machine-utilisation target (e.g., ≥ 80 % of planned order quantity)?
       A: A minimum utilisation floor of 70 % of the shift's planned order quantity is enforced to prevent ratio-gaming through deliberate under-production.
    4. operator_fatigue_index (enforcement timing: instantaneous vs. end-of-shift)
       Q: Should the fatigue constraint (≤ 0.4) be enforced at every decision epoch throughout the shift, or only as a time-weighted average at shift end — because a task-sequence that spikes to 0.55 for 30 minutes before recovering could satisfy an end-of-shift average of 0.38?
       A: Fatigue is enforced as a hard ceiling at every scheduling interval (≤ 15-minute resolution); any action that would cause the instantaneous index to exceed 0.4 is rejected.
    5. ambient_noise_level (TWA-only vs. peak constraint)
       Q: The 80 dB(A) limit is defined as a time-weighted average; should there also be an instantaneous peak ceiling (e.g., 110 dB(A) per ISO 9612 / OSHA 1910.95) to prevent short bursts of high-noise operations that remain within the TWA but still pose hearing-damage risk?
       A: An additional instantaneous peak constraint of 110 dB(A) is imposed alongside the 80 dB(A) TWA limit, consistent with ISO 9612 dual-criterion practice.

