    1. priority_order vs _raw_priority_order conflict
       Q: When maximising resource efficiency would require pushing operator fatigue toward the 0.4 ceiling, which objective should win: the stated 'HumanWellbeing' top priority (suggesting fatigue must stay well below 0.4) or the raw priority order that lists resource_efficiency first? These two encodings are contradictory and will produce different schedules.
       A: Honour the explicit priority_order value 'HumanWellbeing'; treat fatigue and noise hard constraints as lexicographically superior to any resource_efficiency gain, so the optimiser never trades wellbeing headroom for efficiency.
    2. resource_efficiency (no upper bound on total resource consumption)
       Q: Is there a per-shift ceiling on absolute resource use—e.g. energy ≤ X kWh or labour-hours ≤ Y—independent of the efficiency ratio? Without one the optimiser may increase total resource consumption by 30–40 % if doing so raises delivered value proportionally more, which could exceed budget, capacity, or safety limits.
       A: Cap total resource consumption at 110 % of the shift's rolling 30-day historical baseline to prevent runaway absolute escalation while still allowing efficiency improvements.
    3. resource_scope aggregation formula
       Q: How should energy, labour, and material be collapsed into a single resource denominator for the efficiency ratio—equal weight, monetary cost at today's rates, or a fixed formula? The choice fundamentally changes which trade-offs the optimiser favours (e.g. cost-weighting incentivises substituting cheap energy for expensive labour).
       A: Use cost-weighted aggregation based on the site's most recent monthly cost rates; if rates are unavailable, apply equal fractional weighting across the three resource categories until the manager supplies weights.
    4. operator_fatigue threshold semantics (time-average vs instantaneous ceiling)
       Q: Is the 0.4 fatigue limit a time-averaged value across the full 8-hour shift, or an instantaneous ceiling that must never be exceeded at any moment? Interpreted as a shift average, the optimiser may legally schedule high-intensity bursts (fatigue > 0.4) offset by rest periods, creating real safety exposure despite formal compliance.
       A: Treat 0.4 as an instantaneous hard ceiling—fatigue must not exceed this value at any sampled point during the shift—to maximise worker safety and align with the ergonomic-standard basis stated in assumptions.
    5. noise_level measurement (Leq averaging vs peak instantaneous limit)
       Q: Does the 80 dB limit apply only to the continuous equivalent level (Leq) averaged over the shift, or must instantaneous peak noise also stay below a separate cap (commonly 85 dB peak under occupational health guidance)? Leq averaging alone can mask harmful burst exposures that the optimiser might cluster into short windows.
       A: Enforce 80 dB as the Leq limit and additionally apply an 85 dB instantaneous peak cap at the operator workstation, consistent with standard occupational health practice, until the manager confirms a different peak threshold.

